7</year><volume>20</volume><issue>6</issue><fpage>894</fpage><lpage>899</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><label>24</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group><name><surname>Sano</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Shiga</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Tamura</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Metabolism and glucose kinetics in sheep fed plantain and orchard grass and exposed to cold</article-title><source>NZ J Agric Res</source><year>2002</year><volume>45</volume><fpage>171</fpage><lpage>177</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B25"><label>25</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group><name><surname>Rodriguez</surname><given-names>NR</given-names></name><name><surname>Prigge</surname><given-names>EC</given-names></name><name><surname>Lough</surname><given-names>DS</given-names></name><name><surname>Hoover</surname><given-names>WH</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Glucogenic and hormonal responses to abomasal casein and ruminal volatile fatty acid infusions in lactating goats</article-title><source>J Dairy Sci</source><year>1985</year><volume>68</volume><fpage>1968</fpage><lpage>1975</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3900158</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B26"><label>26</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group><name><surname>Sabine</surname><given-names>JR</given-names></name><name><surname>Johnson</surname><given-names>BC</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Acetate metabolism in the ruminant</article-title><source>J Biol Chem</source><year>1964</year><volume>239</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>89</fpage><lpage>93</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14114878</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B27"><label>27</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group><name><surname>Bagley</surname><given-names>CP</given-names></name><name><surname>Feazel</surname><given-names>JI</given-names></name><name><surname>Morrison</surname><given-names>DG</given-names></name><name><surname>Lucas</surname><given-names>DM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Effects of salinomycin on ruminal characteristics and performance of grazing beef steers</article-title><source>J Anim Sci</source><year>1988</year><volume>66</volume><fpage>792</fpage><lpage>797</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3378935</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B28"><label>28</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group><name><surname>Reffett-Stabel</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Spears</surname><given-names>JW</given-names></name><name><surname>Harvey</surname><given-names>RW</given-names></name><name><surname>Lucas</surname><given-names>DM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Salinomycin and lasalocid effects on growth rate, mineral metabolism and ruminal fermentation in steers</article-title><source>J Anim Sci</source><year>1989</year><volume>67</volume><fpage>2735</fpage><lpage>2742</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2808174</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B29"><label>29</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group><name><surname>Sunagawa</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Otani</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Hagino</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Takahashi</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Otomo</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Shoji</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Ambo</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Tsuda</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Kinetics of exogenous and endogenous acetate metabolism in sheep exposed to cold environment</article-title><source>Jpn J Zootech Sci</source><year>1986</year><volume>57</volume><fpage>201</fpage><lpage>208</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B30"><label>30</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>SD</given-names></name><name><surname>Williams</surname><given-names>WF</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Acetate turnover rate as determined by two superimposed techniques</article-title><source>J Dairy Sci</source><year>1962</year><volume>45</volume><fpage>517</fpage></citation></ref></ref-list><bio id="bio1"><p><bold>Dr. Mohammad Al-Mamun </bold>is an Associate Prof., Dept. of Animal Nutrition, Bangladesh Agricultural University, and currently working as a researcher in the Dept. of Animal Sciences, Iwate University, Japan. His research is focusing on the use of natural herbs to modulate intermediate nutrients; glucose, amino acids, short chain fatty acids, metabolism in ruminants. Dr. Al-Mamun received Ph.D. from Iwate University in 2008 under the supervision of Prof. Dr. Hiroaki Sano. Dr. Al-Mamun is a very young scientist with excellent academic as well as research careers with University Prize and Gold Medal Award (2003 and 2005, for academic excellence, Bangladesh Agricultural University), Dean Award, and President Award (2008, Iwate University in recognition of the excellence of Ph. D. research), Japanese Society of Animal Science Outstanding Young Scientist Award (2009, for outstanding and innovative research work). Besides, Dr. Al-Mamun was the grantee of Asian Youth Fellowship (2003, Japanese Ministry of Foreign Affair through ASIA SEED Institute), Japanese Government (Monbukagakusho) Scholarship (2004), the 12<sup>th</sup> AAAP Animal Science Congress Scholarship (2006, Korea), and JSPS Fellowship for 2 years (2008, Japanese Society for the Promotion of Science).</p></bio><bio id="bio2"><p><bold>Dr. Hiroaki Sano</bold> is a Prof. and head, Lab. of Animal Nutrition and Physiology, Dept. of Animal Sciences, Iwate University, Japan. Dr. Sano also the president of Field Science Center of Faculty of Agriculture of Iwate University. Dr. Sano has a special interest in blood glucose, amino acid and acetate metabolisms and control in ruminants.</p></bio></back><floats-wrap><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Proximate composition and polyphenol contents of hop-residues (<italic>Humulus lupulus</italic> L.), and mixed hay (MH) of orchardgrass (<italic>Dactylis glomerata</italic> L.) and reed canarygrass (<italic>Phalaris arundinacea</italic> L.) (60:40).</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead valign="top"><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Hop</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">MH</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Dry matter (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">84.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">91.9</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Crude protein (% DM)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">17.6</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">10.2</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Crude fiber (% DM)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">13.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">28.8</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Ether extract (% DM)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">9.7</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.0</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Crude ash (% DM)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">29.3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">11.7</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Nitrogen-free extract (% DM)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">30.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">46.3</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Polyphenol (mg/g DM)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">ND<sup>*</sup></td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn><p><sup>*</sup>ND= not detected; DM=dry matter</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Effect of hop (<italic>Humulus lupulus</italic> L.) on ruminal characteristics at 2 h after feeding in sheep.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead valign="top"><tr><th rowspan="2" colspan="1">Rumen parameters</th><th 